#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199318} :activity/id #uuid "741f534f-c497-52b3-a525-05d9bf7f9e8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/id #uuid "37969d71-54e0-5479-8db0-03f8bc2bcfc3" :topic/math-academy-id 4160} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198242 :question/id #uuid "366c826f-6f15-5a21-9eaa-d74b6e4d8833" :question/math-academy-id "e-9575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372118}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "2c5a36f7-0cf5-56e0-9ad2-156e162e6abd" :step/math-academy-id 35868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199478 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nwhich is defined on an open set $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ Recall that a function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$P = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nIf a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y),$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ is called a **source-free vector field**.\n\nSource-free vector fields have some interesting properties, summarized below:\n\n- *The divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at any point of $D$ is zero:* $$\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$$\n- *The*flux*of $\\mathbf{F}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero:* $$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$$\n- *Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that* $$\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s.$$\n\nStream functions for source-free vector fields play a similar role to potential functions for conservative vector fields. So it's worth comparing them side-by-side.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" style=\"border:1px solid black; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 4mm; margin-top: 4mm\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center\">Source-Free</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\" width=\"450\">Stream Function $\\psi$\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Relationship between $\\psi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\mathbf F$ is tangential to $\\psi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Divergence is zero\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Flux is zero along closed curves\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Flux integrals are path independent\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td width=\"30\"></td>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" style=\"border:1px solid black; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 4mm; margin-top: 4mm\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center\">Conservative</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\" width=\"450\">Potential Function $\\phi$\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf i +  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Relationship between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\mathbf F$ is perpendicular to $\\phi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">(Scalar) curl is zero\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\"> $\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Circulation is zero along closed curves\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}= 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Line integrals are path independent\n</td><td style=\"text-align:center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :tutorial/id #uuid "69005432-5588-5ac3-bf47-9dd734bb6953" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7703 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e2deeb1-79f4-5a70-897d-e88547dd9d33" :step/math-academy-id 44699 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198045 :question/id #uuid "7a42fb45-176b-5550-a206-41a92859987b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372195}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "c4b926bf-0635-5002-9a4a-976c76662c96" :step/math-academy-id 44701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199485 :tutorial/content "Let's use what we know about source-free vector fields to calculate the flux of\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\,\\mathbf{i}-2x\\,\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nacross the curve $C,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d6/d6adda2809af5d49c6b3dd0e81eae913dcdf02ceec0ae919fda0011d8a3dad2b.png)\n\nFirst, notice that this is a source-free vector field, since\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x) \\\\ & =0+0 \\\\ & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "447855fc-5a5c-5450-99a6-91de0ed2203b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7751 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b606814b-0712-51ca-ad68-9fac2299c246" :step/math-academy-id 44869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199265}}] :activity/title "Source-Free Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198492}]}
